          "type": "practice",
          "title": "Map the Whole Book Onto a Timeline",
          "content": "Build the full timeline of this book yourself. Using the dates and connections covered across all fifteen lessons, place each item below at its approximate point on a single timeline stretching from roughly 1900 to the 1960s, and draw one arrow from each item to whichever later item it most directly influenced.\n\n**Items to place**: Husserl's founding phenomenological work (early 1900s) • Heidegger's *Being and Time* (1927) • Heidegger's \"What Is Metaphysics?\" (1929) and Carnap's reply (1932) • Heidegger's Nazi Party membership (1933) • Sartre's *Being and Nothingness* (1943) • Camus's *The Myth of Sisyphus* (1942) and *The Stranger* (1942) • de Beauvoir's *The Ethics of Ambiguity* (1947) • Heidegger's \"Letter on Humanism\" (1947) • de Beauvoir's *The Second Sex* (1949) • Merleau-Ponty's *Phenomenology of Perception* (1945) • the Sartre-Camus rupture (1952) • Camus's *The Rebel* (1951) • Sartre's *Critique of Dialectical Reason* (1960).\n\nThen answer in your own words: which single event on your timeline most changes how you read everything that came before it, and why? There is a strong case for 1933 (Heidegger's Nazi membership, forcing every later reader to hold his philosophy and his politics together) and an equally strong case for 1952 (the Sartre-Camus rupture, revealing the movement's real internal fractures). Defend whichever you pick.",
